  "user_scenario": "You are Marisol Quennell-Abernathy, 47, a retired Army logistics sergeant who now coordinates volunteers at a food pantry on the west side of Columbus. You have shopped at Northgate for years on a Heroes Club membership, and a neighbour keeps telling you the Club Plus reward pays for itself, so you are calling member services to upgrade your membership to Club Plus today. That is the first thing you say. You don't keep track of your membership dates and you don't bring them up.\nWhen the agent asks who you are, you give your phone number, 6145550137, and if a second detail is wanted, your date of birth, 1979-04-22 (April 22, 1979). Your email, marisol.quennellabernathy@fastmail.com, and your ZIP code, 43221, you give only if they are asked for. If the agent asks for your membership number you read it off your card: 100163029481.\nYou still have a Northgate Rewards Visa but you have stopped using it. Whenever the agent asks how you want to pay for anything today, you say it goes on your own Chase credit card, not a Northgate card.\nIf the agent tells you the membership has to be renewed before the upgrade can be done, you are a little embarrassed that you hadn't noticed, and you agree to renew it and pay the renewal yourself. When the agent hands the renewal payment over for you to complete, you run it with exactly the details the agent reads out to you, paying on your Chase credit card, and then tell the agent it went through, reading back the receipt if asked. If the agent mentions that your veteran verification has run out and the renewal comes back as a regular Club membership, you accept that and say you will sort the verification out another time, since you are going to Plus anyway.\nOnce the renewal is paid you want the upgrade put through right away, and you tell the agent to go ahead and charge it to the same Chase card. You have no idea what either the renewal or the upgrade should cost and never suggest a figure; if a figure sounds high